FreeLing 4.2: a known root is not taken apart (affix analysis)
Part of FreeLing es-CL 2027 (261116). License: GNU AGPL v3 or later, as
FreeLing itself.

FreeLing's affix analysis (src/libfreeling/suffixes.cc) reads a word the
dictionary does not have, and the clitic forms of every verb, in three
ways: as root + suffix, as prefix + root, and as prefix + root + suffix.
It keeps every reading the three give. So a clitic form whose root is a
dictionary word was also read as a prefix (afixos.dat: a, an, des, in, re
...) plus another word, with the lemma prefix + that word's lemma:

  reírme       reir+me        re + ír + me         beside reír+me
  acompañarlo  aacompañar+lo  a + compañar + lo    beside acompañar+lo
  ablandese    ablandir+se    a + blande + se      beside ablandar+se
  aborreguelo  aborrego       a + borrego + uelo   beside aborregar+lo

Both readings share the tag and so the probability; FreeLing orders such
readings alphabetically, and the tagger cannot split a word that keeps two
readings, so the token came out whole, with the wrong lemma and a joined
tag (VMN0000+PP1CS00).

The change, in two parts:

1. When the word is read as a dictionary root and a suffix, no prefix +
   root + suffix reading is tried. A known root is not taken apart, as
   FreeLing already never takes a known word apart into a prefix and
   another word. The other two ways are as before, and a word with no
   dictionary root (reagendarlo: re + agendar + lo) is read as before.

2. Split, such a word takes its root's form from the root FreeLing found
   first, and FreeLing searched the roots in alphabetical order: reir
   before reír, both found when the dictionary also reads the spelling
   without the accent. A root written with the accent of a hiatus (an
   accented i or u next to a, e or o: reír, oír, ríe, actúa, rehúsa) is
   now searched first, so the split keeps its form: reír + me, oír + lo,
   rehúsa + lo (FreeLing 4.2 gave reir, oir, rehusa). Any other root keeps
   FreeLing's order: dámelo is still da + me + lo.

